    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 5\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[29, 20, 13], [6, 5, 26], [7, 24, 10], [17, 24, 26], [23, 15, 9]]\n    machine_assignments = [[2, 1, 0], [0, 2, 1], [0, 1, 2], [1, 0, 2], [2, 1, 0]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Coordinated production schedule across specialized workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized workstations. We are preparing to run a set of customer orders, each of which follows a defined series of operations across the machines. Every operation has a designated machine and a known run time. Because these routings overlap on shared equipment, day-to-day sequencing decisions directly influence completion dates and overall equipment utilization.\n\nScheduling challenge\nWe need a coordinated, plant-wide sequence of all operations that respects each job\u2019s routing while managing contention on shared machines. The timing of one step constrains when the next step in the same job can begin, and processing times vary from short to long. Without a carefully constructed sequence, machines may sit idle while downstream steps wait, or bottlenecks can form where several operations compete for the same workstation. The task is to align these interdependent operations so that flow is smooth and the total finish time is as short as possible.\n\nOperating requirements\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process only one operation at a time.\n- Fixed job order: For every job, operations must be completed in their specified sequence; a subsequent step cannot start before the previous one finishes.\n- Machine specificity and duration: Each operation must run on its designated workstation for its known duration.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a feasible and efficient schedule that completes all jobs in the shortest overall time (minimizes makespan), while maintaining high machine utilization and minimizing waiting between sequential operations.\n\nNext steps\n- Build an initial feasible sequence that satisfies all operating requirements.\n- Evaluate overall completion time and machine load profiles to identify bottlenecks and idle gaps.\n- Iterate on the sequence to reduce the total finish time and balance work across machines.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 5\n- Number of machines: 3\n- Processing times by operation: Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 3 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 29, Op2: 20, Op3: 13]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 6, Op2: 5, Op3: 26]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 7, Op2: 24, Op3: 10]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 17, Op2: 24, Op3: 26]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: 23, Op2: 15, Op3: 9]\n- Machine assignments by operation: Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 2\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 1]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 0]",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Coordinated production schedule across specialized workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized workstations. We are preparing to run a set of customer orders, each of which follows a defined series of operations across the machines. Every operation has a designated machine and a known run time. Because these routings overlap on shared equipment, day-to-day sequencing decisions directly influence completion dates and overall equipment utilization.\n\nScheduling challenge\nWe need a coordinated, plant-wide sequence of all operations that respects each job\u2019s routing while managing contention on shared machines. The timing of one step constrains when the next step in the same job can begin, and processing times vary from short to long. Without a carefully constructed sequence, machines may sit idle while downstream steps wait, or bottlenecks can form where several operations compete for the same workstation. The task is to align these interdependent operations so that flow is smooth and the total finish time is as short as possible.\n\nOperating requirements\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process only one operation at a time.\n- Fixed job order: For every job, operations must be completed in their specified sequence; a subsequent step cannot start before the previous one finishes.\n- Machine specificity and duration: Each operation must run on its designated workstation for its known duration.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a feasible and efficient schedule that completes all jobs in the shortest overall time (minimizes makespan), while maintaining high machine utilization and minimizing waiting between sequential operations.\n\nNext steps\n- Build an initial feasible sequence that satisfies all operating requirements.\n- Evaluate overall completion time and machine load profiles to identify bottlenecks and idle gaps.\n- Iterate on the sequence to reduce the total finish time and balance work across machines.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines: {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Processing times by operation: {PROCESSING_TIMES}\n- Machine assignments by operation: {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}"
